# Line endings are contractual for the distributed scripts:
#   - .sh must be LF everywhere.  Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which
#     would rewrite a cloned copy to CRLF; every shell variable would then carry a
#     trailing CR and the script dies on Linux with "No such file or directory".
#   - .bat must stay byte-identical to the committed CRLF form.  The documented usage
#     downloads it straight from the raw URL into cmd.exe, so the stored blob itself has
#     to carry CRLF -- normalizing it to LF on commit ships a broken batch file (cmd.exe
#     mis-parses LF-only labels and blocks).
#   - .sha256 files must stay byte-exact, so the published digest keeps matching the
#     binary it names.
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.bat    -text
*.sha256 -text
